Why Device Performance Depends on Controlled Film Growth
Power converters need specific layer thicknesses, dopant profiles and defect controls to withstand electric stress. Optical connectivity uses III–V stacks with precise bandgap and interface properties. Analog and RF manufacturers face still different mobility and noise requirements. These demands drive specialization in epitaxy rather than a universal wafer specification.

Uniformity across a full wafer and across different production runs is a core commercial hurdle. A slight change in growth temperature, precursor availability or defect density can affect downstream device acceptance. The customer often qualifies the entire layer stack and growth process before allowing a new source into production.

