Epitaxial qualification spans more than a coating thickness
Engineered crystalline films must preserve the proper lattice relationship to their starting substrate. Engineers therefore evaluate defects, compositional abruptness, electrical doping and interface behavior, often with several metrology methods. A supplier promising a larger wafer diameter or faster growth rate still must prove the film meets the application’s accepted defect and electrical criteria.

Process choice follows the target material system
Silicon vapor-phase growth, metal-organic routes and molecular beam epitaxy offer different strengths and costs. One approach may support a mature high-volume silicon product, whereas another is necessary for precisely engineered compound heterostructures. Market opportunities must therefore be interpreted through the actual growth process and customer device, not just an aggregate wafer forecast.

